1
00:00:00,200 --> 00:00:03,133
that's a stem tip 3 to 4 inches long

2
00:00:03,233 --> 00:00:07,600
by spring it can be a whole new geranium and it costs you nothing

3
00:00:08,566 --> 00:00:11,000
frost is coming for the ones on your porch

4
00:00:11,033 --> 00:00:15,166
you don't have to haul every pot inside or buy new plants next year

5
00:00:15,166 --> 00:00:20,000
a sharp knife a small pot a sunny window that's the whole kit

6
00:00:20,900 --> 00:00:24,666
but one guide adds a step for geraniums that the others leave out

7
00:00:24,666 --> 00:00:28,566
it happens before the cutting ever touches the soil skip it

8
00:00:28,566 --> 00:00:30,400
and you may leave the fresh cut open

9
00:00:30,400 --> 00:00:33,000
to the kind of rot that turns a cutting black

10
00:00:34,633 --> 00:00:35,933
start at the top

11
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:38,066
find a healthy stem

12
00:00:38,066 --> 00:00:40,966
and follow it out to the tip the very end of the shoot

13
00:00:41,033 --> 00:00:42,900
measure down 3 to 4 inches

14
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:46,500
that's where the cut goes with a sharp knife or clean pruners

15
00:00:46,666 --> 00:00:48,400
then the lower leaves come off

16
00:00:48,400 --> 00:00:50,900
so nothing leafy ends up buried in the mix

17
00:00:51,000 --> 00:00:52,966
two quick checks come first though

18
00:00:53,966 --> 00:00:55,733
that's it that's a cutting

19
00:00:55,833 --> 00:00:58,566
3 to 4 inches from the tip is the whole rule

20
00:00:58,666 --> 00:01:01,833
hold it up and it'll look small almost too small

21
00:01:01,833 --> 00:01:04,333
small is fine no special tools

22
00:01:04,433 --> 00:01:07,200
if you can use a kitchen knife you can do this

23
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:10,200
here's why the leaves come off

24
00:01:10,233 --> 00:01:13,333
anything leafy under the surface sits in damp mix

25
00:01:13,433 --> 00:01:15,900
and damp mix is where rot likes to start

26
00:01:16,766 --> 00:01:21,066
why the tip the research doesn't say so we won't pretend to know

27
00:01:21,100 --> 00:01:23,966
what matters is the length and now you have it

28
00:01:24,766 --> 00:01:26,600
quick word about the plant itself

29
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:30,100
the red and pink ones on your porch are tender geraniums

30
00:01:30,266 --> 00:01:32,300
their proper name is pelargonium

31
00:01:32,366 --> 00:01:34,900
and geranium is just the nickname that stuck

32
00:01:34,900 --> 00:01:37,466
the true geraniums are a different group of plants

33
00:01:37,466 --> 00:01:39,400
and this video isn't about those

34
00:01:40,300 --> 00:01:44,666
tender means a hard freeze finishes them the rule is simple

35
00:01:44,700 --> 00:01:49,000
get the plant or a piece of it indoors before the first frost

36
00:01:49,100 --> 00:01:51,100
and don't wait until the last minute

37
00:01:52,066 --> 00:01:54,900
there are three ways to bring a geranium through winter

38
00:01:54,900 --> 00:01:56,133
keep the whole plant

39
00:01:56,233 --> 00:01:59,500
store it bare root in a paper bag or take cuttings

40
00:01:59,666 --> 00:02:03,100
only one of the three gives you more plants than you started with

41
00:02:04,233 --> 00:02:06,566
why a cutting and not the whole pot

42
00:02:06,900 --> 00:02:10,700
because a whole plant kept indoors wants a bright spot all winter

43
00:02:10,900 --> 00:02:13,466
a cutting wants a corner of one window sill

44
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:18,000
and the plant itself costs you nothing not a nursery tag in sight

45
00:02:18,900 --> 00:02:21,933
if you've bought the same geranium spring after spring

46
00:02:22,066 --> 00:02:24,666
you already know what it costs to let them freeze

47
00:02:25,700 --> 00:02:28,466
before you watch another minute do one thing

48
00:02:28,466 --> 00:02:32,666
take one cutting today just one from your healthiest plant

49
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:36,166
not this second though stay with me a few minutes first

50
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:38,366
if frost is in this week's forecast

51
00:02:38,466 --> 00:02:42,333
make it the first thing you do tomorrow morning one is enough

52
00:02:42,366 --> 00:02:45,366
the first cutting shows you what's easy and what's fussy

53
00:02:45,433 --> 00:02:49,000
everything else in this video is about making that first one count

54
00:02:49,966 --> 00:02:52,766
look up your area's average first frost date

55
00:02:52,833 --> 00:02:54,500
it's different in every zone

56
00:02:54,566 --> 00:02:57,533
and it's the one date on this calendar you can't move

57
00:02:58,433 --> 00:03:00,266
the cut itself takes seconds

58
00:03:00,266 --> 00:03:04,066
what happens before and after it is where cuttings are won or lost

59
00:03:04,900 --> 00:03:07,900
don't rush to the knife walk out to the plant

60
00:03:08,033 --> 00:03:10,466
and take a slow look at the stem you've picked

61
00:03:10,666 --> 00:03:12,866
turn it over look at the base

62
00:03:13,066 --> 00:03:15,466
look at everything growing along its length

63
00:03:16,266 --> 00:03:17,900
because there's one thing on that

64
00:03:17,900 --> 00:03:20,766
stem that has to come off before it goes anywhere

65
00:03:21,900 --> 00:03:24,966
before the stem look at the plant it's growing on

66
00:03:25,033 --> 00:03:27,966
a cutting from a sick plant starts life sick

67
00:03:29,200 --> 00:03:32,500
go out to the porch in good light and check three things

68
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:36,733
first spots or discolored patches on the leaves or the stems

69
00:03:36,966 --> 00:03:41,400
second any wilting or any rot third insects

70
00:03:41,600 --> 00:03:43,600
hold a leaf up and look underneath

71
00:03:43,766 --> 00:03:45,600
run your eyes along the stem

72
00:03:45,766 --> 00:03:48,466
a flashlight helps with the underside of the leaves

73
00:03:48,600 --> 00:03:53,000
no tools needed for this step just your eyes and a little patience

74
00:03:53,866 --> 00:03:57,166
if a plant shows any of those it doesn't get picked

75
00:03:57,300 --> 00:03:59,666
and do this check on a calm afternoon

76
00:03:59,866 --> 00:04:02,266
not the night the frost warning comes in

77
00:04:02,400 --> 00:04:06,866
check for pests before anything comes indoors whole plant or cutting

78
00:04:07,866 --> 00:04:09,566
that's mistake No. 1

79
00:04:09,633 --> 00:04:12,900
taking cuttings from the plant that looks like it needs rescuing

80
00:04:13,033 --> 00:04:16,000
it feels like the generous thing to do you know

81
00:04:16,066 --> 00:04:19,300
but a rescue and a cutting are two different jobs

82
00:04:19,400 --> 00:04:21,533
only healthy plants go forward

83
00:04:22,400 --> 00:04:26,833
picture a row of geraniums along the porch rail most of them look good

84
00:04:26,833 --> 00:04:29,400
one at the end has a few brown edged leaves

85
00:04:29,400 --> 00:04:31,366
and a stem that's starting to droop

86
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:35,300
it's the one you feel sorry for it's also the one you walk past

87
00:04:36,233 --> 00:04:39,800
that plant isn't a lost cause it just isn't a donor

88
00:04:39,866 --> 00:04:41,933
and keep it away from the healthy ones

89
00:04:42,000 --> 00:04:44,133
don't carry it inside with the rest

90
00:04:44,233 --> 00:04:46,300
keep it apart or let it go

91
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:48,633
now a safety note

92
00:04:48,633 --> 00:04:51,300
because we're about to be trimming and handling these

93
00:04:51,566 --> 00:04:56,466
the ASPCA lists pelargonium as toxic to dogs cats and horses

94
00:04:56,800 --> 00:04:58,533
the signs include vomiting

95
00:04:58,566 --> 00:05:03,000
loss of appetite a tired quiet pet and skin irritation

96
00:05:03,300 --> 00:05:06,300
keep the trimmings off the floor and off the counter

97
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:08,733
where a pet or a child can reach them

98
00:05:09,400 --> 00:05:11,500
if your pet has already taken a nibble

99
00:05:11,600 --> 00:05:14,466
call your vet or an animal poison control line

100
00:05:14,466 --> 00:05:16,366
and tell them what the plant was

101
00:05:17,633 --> 00:05:21,333
OK healthy plant pets covered on to the stem

102
00:05:22,200 --> 00:05:26,300
pick a shoot that's growing strong with a clear tip you're only taking

103
00:05:26,300 --> 00:05:27,066
the end of it

104
00:05:27,066 --> 00:05:30,166
so the rest of the plant keeps its leaves and keeps growing

105
00:05:30,300 --> 00:05:34,200
that's the lazy gardener's way take a little leave the rest

106
00:05:35,066 --> 00:05:38,733
take your time here a good cutting is a calm decision

107
00:05:38,766 --> 00:05:40,700
and it's never a frantic one

108
00:05:40,766 --> 00:05:44,900
if you can't decide between two stems take the sturdier one

109
00:05:45,700 --> 00:05:48,900
a healthy plant and a healthy stem still aren't enough

110
00:05:48,900 --> 00:05:51,266
a cutting can fail for a reason that's sitting

111
00:05:51,266 --> 00:05:53,000
right there on the stem itself

112
00:05:53,100 --> 00:05:56,100
not on the plant not in the soil on the stem

113
00:05:57,100 --> 00:06:01,733
the step from the start comes later but first the thing on that stem

114
00:06:02,900 --> 00:06:04,266
it's the flowers

115
00:06:05,300 --> 00:06:06,700
pinch off the flowers

116
00:06:06,700 --> 00:06:10,333
and any seed pods too before the cutting goes anywhere

117
00:06:10,366 --> 00:06:15,933
thumb and finger will do it it takes a few seconds pinch pinch done

118
00:06:16,800 --> 00:06:20,933
think of it this way a stem with blooms on it is busy making blooms

119
00:06:21,166 --> 00:06:23,500
you want that stem busy making roots

120
00:06:23,766 --> 00:06:27,566
take the flowers off and the energy has one place left to go

121
00:06:28,433 --> 00:06:32,566
that's mistake No. 2 leaving the blooms on because they're pretty

122
00:06:32,600 --> 00:06:34,500
a flower looks great on a plant

123
00:06:34,566 --> 00:06:36,333
it does a cutting no favors

124
00:06:37,433 --> 00:06:42,366
next the leaves you've taken the lower ones off leave a few at the top

125
00:06:42,400 --> 00:06:44,700
if your tip has a big crown of leaves

126
00:06:44,766 --> 00:06:46,800
trimming a little is a judgment call

127
00:06:47,566 --> 00:06:50,866
and the length again because you may have seen a different number

128
00:06:50,900 --> 00:06:54,466
a popular video with more than 700,000 views

129
00:06:54,566 --> 00:06:57,000
cuts its geranium cuttings at 6 inches

130
00:06:57,266 --> 00:07:01,700
6 is nearly double what the university sources say which is 3 to 4

131
00:07:01,766 --> 00:07:04,900
maybe it worked for them start with 3 to four anyway

132
00:07:05,666 --> 00:07:07,333
one part holds steady

133
00:07:07,400 --> 00:07:10,666
three university sites land on the same three points

134
00:07:10,766 --> 00:07:15,566
a tip cutting bright light no direct sun hold on to those three

135
00:07:16,366 --> 00:07:20,733
look at what you're holding a clean bare tip no flowers

136
00:07:20,866 --> 00:07:25,100
a few leaves at the top small tidy and ready to go

137
00:07:25,866 --> 00:07:29,300
what it goes into decides whether it roots or rots

138
00:07:29,433 --> 00:07:32,733
and the step from the start is waiting just behind it

139
00:07:33,800 --> 00:07:35,700
a cutting doesn't ask for much

140
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:38,900
it needs a pot a mix and a little patience

141
00:07:39,833 --> 00:07:43,700
picture the kitchen counter on a Saturday morning a small pot

142
00:07:43,766 --> 00:07:48,166
a bag of perlite a cup of coffee nothing else is on the list

143
00:07:49,700 --> 00:07:50,833
start with the pot

144
00:07:50,833 --> 00:07:54,900
any small pot will do as long as it has drainage holes in the bottom

145
00:07:55,066 --> 00:07:57,200
drainage holes are non negotiable

146
00:07:57,266 --> 00:08:00,100
water with nowhere to go sits around the cut end

147
00:08:00,100 --> 00:08:02,533
and a wet cut end is where rot starts

148
00:08:03,500 --> 00:08:07,500
now the mix you have choices vermiculite works

149
00:08:07,600 --> 00:08:11,466
so does perlite on its own or perlite with a little peat moss

150
00:08:11,766 --> 00:08:15,800
sand is on the list and so is a bagged rooting mix from the store

151
00:08:15,866 --> 00:08:20,533
if peat bothers you you don't need it perlite or sand alone is fine

152
00:08:21,466 --> 00:08:24,333
skip the dirt from the yard use a clean mix

153
00:08:25,266 --> 00:08:28,233
what about a glass of water plenty of you will ask

154
00:08:28,233 --> 00:08:31,166
we didn't see water compared with mix for geraniums

155
00:08:31,233 --> 00:08:32,766
so we won't call a winner

156
00:08:33,600 --> 00:08:36,133
1 optional extra rooting powder

157
00:08:36,200 --> 00:08:37,800
it's a quick dip of the base

158
00:08:37,866 --> 00:08:40,500
and you'll use it after the next step if you have it

159
00:08:40,566 --> 00:08:43,100
keep the powder out of reach of kids and pets

160
00:08:44,166 --> 00:08:47,100
on to the money the plant costs you nothing

161
00:08:47,100 --> 00:08:49,966
the pot and the mix might not cost much either

162
00:08:50,166 --> 00:08:52,000
maybe you already have a small pot

163
00:08:52,000 --> 00:08:54,100
and half a bag of something in the shed

164
00:08:54,400 --> 00:08:57,300
compare that with the rack at the nursery next spring

165
00:08:58,633 --> 00:09:00,966
this part is for anyone who has watched a cutting

166
00:09:00,966 --> 00:09:02,366
turn black at the base

167
00:09:03,433 --> 00:09:06,133
that black base has a name blackleg

168
00:09:06,200 --> 00:09:09,800
it starts as a brown water soaked rot at the bottom of the cutting

169
00:09:09,900 --> 00:09:12,600
then it turns coal black and climbs the stem

170
00:09:12,766 --> 00:09:16,466
greenhouse guidance says it can run 3 to 4 inches up the stem

171
00:09:16,566 --> 00:09:20,400
about the length of your whole cutting and it kills the cutting fast

172
00:09:21,233 --> 00:09:26,066
the fixes are the plain ones you just heard a clean mix drainage holes

173
00:09:26,266 --> 00:09:28,000
and if a cutting does go black

174
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:30,766
throw it out even one that already has roots

175
00:09:31,633 --> 00:09:35,533
the pot is ready the mix is ready don't plant anything yet

176
00:09:36,366 --> 00:09:39,600
lay the cutting on a clean paper towel and hold on

177
00:09:39,666 --> 00:09:43,700
one step comes before the pot and it's the one the hook held back

178
00:09:44,766 --> 00:09:46,133
the step is simple

179
00:09:46,266 --> 00:09:50,066
let the cut end heal before the cutting goes in the mix

180
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:52,600
it's the one from the start

181
00:09:52,600 --> 00:09:55,533
the one that happens before the cutting touches the soil

182
00:09:56,500 --> 00:09:59,866
here's why this works a cut stem is an open wound

183
00:09:59,966 --> 00:10:01,300
left to sit for a while

184
00:10:01,300 --> 00:10:04,933
that wound seals itself with a layer of new tissue called callus

185
00:10:05,066 --> 00:10:07,366
and that layer helps keep disease out

186
00:10:07,566 --> 00:10:09,333
remember the black base from earlier

187
00:10:09,433 --> 00:10:12,300
an open cut is where that kind of rot can get in

188
00:10:13,400 --> 00:10:15,733
now watch what the universities do with it

189
00:10:16,433 --> 00:10:19,733
Illinois Extension names Pelargonium as an exception

190
00:10:19,800 --> 00:10:21,800
it shouldn't go straight into the mix

191
00:10:22,000 --> 00:10:24,800
let the cut air dry and heal over first

192
00:10:25,700 --> 00:10:28,600
Texas A&M says the same thing a different way

193
00:10:28,633 --> 00:10:33,366
lay the cuttings on damp sand in heavy shade for about three hours

194
00:10:33,400 --> 00:10:37,166
healing before planting it says helps reduce stem rot

195
00:10:37,233 --> 00:10:39,400
that one reads like greenhouse advice

196
00:10:40,433 --> 00:10:42,133
then the quiet ones

197
00:10:42,200 --> 00:10:45,400
Iowa State's cutting instructions don't mention healing

198
00:10:45,566 --> 00:10:49,900
neither do Wisconsin's Penn State's disease guide doesn't either

199
00:10:49,966 --> 00:10:53,366
even Illinois'own overwintering blog skips it

200
00:10:54,200 --> 00:10:57,900
two sources say heal 4 say nothing about it

201
00:10:58,666 --> 00:11:00,766
none of them gives a home timetable

202
00:11:00,966 --> 00:11:04,266
three hours on damp sand is the only number we have

203
00:11:04,266 --> 00:11:06,400
and it comes from the greenhouse advice

204
00:11:06,600 --> 00:11:10,700
and none says how often a cutting survives healed or not

205
00:11:11,300 --> 00:11:12,666
where that leaves us

206
00:11:12,666 --> 00:11:17,733
two sources say heal and none says don't if you want a default heal

207
00:11:17,800 --> 00:11:19,100
or find out for yourself

208
00:11:20,366 --> 00:11:25,333
once one cutting feels easy take 4 heal 2 the way the number says

209
00:11:25,433 --> 00:11:30,600
damp sand on a saucer heavy shade about three hours set a timer

210
00:11:30,666 --> 00:11:35,366
plant the other two straight away same pot same mix same window

211
00:11:35,500 --> 00:11:38,100
label them like a tiny science fair

212
00:11:38,166 --> 00:11:39,900
by the time they've had their weeks

213
00:11:39,900 --> 00:11:42,200
you'll see what happens on your own windowsill

214
00:11:43,266 --> 00:11:46,966
tell us in the comments heal first or straight in

215
00:11:47,066 --> 00:11:49,933
and if you've tried both tell us what happened

216
00:11:50,766 --> 00:11:52,733
you have the step from the start

217
00:11:53,600 --> 00:11:57,066
one more question still hangs over the plant you started with

218
00:11:58,100 --> 00:12:01,200
healed or not now the cutting goes in

219
00:12:02,066 --> 00:12:05,466
dip the base in rooting powder if you have it it's optional

220
00:12:05,466 --> 00:12:08,733
tip a little into a cup first instead of dipping into the jar

221
00:12:08,833 --> 00:12:11,600
follow the label and wash your hands after

222
00:12:11,633 --> 00:12:14,733
poke a hole in the mix with a pencil just a pencil

223
00:12:14,866 --> 00:12:19,333
slide the cutting in only far enough to stand up on its own not deeper

224
00:12:20,266 --> 00:12:22,500
water the mix after the cuttings are in

225
00:12:22,500 --> 00:12:24,600
then let it drain for a few minutes

226
00:12:24,700 --> 00:12:28,100
keep it moist while you wait moist not wet

227
00:12:29,033 --> 00:12:32,566
next cover it a clear plastic bag or a dome

228
00:12:32,566 --> 00:12:34,933
over the pot keeps the leaves from wilting

229
00:12:35,166 --> 00:12:37,100
there are no roots yet to feed them

230
00:12:37,166 --> 00:12:39,733
put it in bright light but out of direct sun

231
00:12:40,800 --> 00:12:45,600
then there's mistake No. 3 sealing the bag air tight and leaving it

232
00:12:45,666 --> 00:12:47,666
mold is the risk under a cover

233
00:12:47,666 --> 00:12:51,966
let some air move open a corner or lift the bag now and then

234
00:12:53,100 --> 00:12:56,266
you may have seen a method with cuttings sealed in a zip bag

235
00:12:56,266 --> 00:12:57,933
never watered for a month

236
00:12:57,966 --> 00:13:02,500
5 out of 5 rooted one person's result and it isn't the advice

237
00:13:02,666 --> 00:13:06,200
cover cuttings so the leaves don't wilt and watch for mold

238
00:13:07,166 --> 00:13:10,466
how long do you wait it's weeks not days

239
00:13:10,500 --> 00:13:14,866
the sources give three different windows and they all include 6 weeks

240
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:18,566
Mark 6 on your calendar then keep counting to eight

241
00:13:19,366 --> 00:13:22,133
they say to pot up once the roots are well developed

242
00:13:22,266 --> 00:13:23,533
you'll have to look

243
00:13:24,300 --> 00:13:25,800
picture week 3

244
00:13:25,866 --> 00:13:29,233
the cutting looks exactly the way it did the day you planted it

245
00:13:29,233 --> 00:13:31,000
you wanna dig it up and check

246
00:13:31,100 --> 00:13:33,533
impatience kicks in right about then

247
00:13:34,366 --> 00:13:38,466
give it its weeks and if a cutting goes dark and soft at the base

248
00:13:38,633 --> 00:13:41,800
don't panic take it out and throw it away

249
00:13:42,666 --> 00:13:47,166
rooted is half the job this is where a good winter can still go wrong

250
00:13:48,366 --> 00:13:50,466
back to the plant you started with

251
00:13:51,100 --> 00:13:52,733
you've got cuttings going

252
00:13:52,766 --> 00:13:55,800
but the plant they came from is still out on the porch

253
00:13:55,966 --> 00:13:58,200
Frost doesn't care how much you like it

254
00:13:58,233 --> 00:14:00,533
first look at where you took the cutting

255
00:14:00,766 --> 00:14:03,400
don't leave a long stub sticking out of the plant

256
00:14:03,466 --> 00:14:06,266
and clear away any fading flowers and leaves

257
00:14:06,466 --> 00:14:08,900
those are the spots where rot can start

258
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:12,533
so what do you do with the plant now there are three answers

259
00:14:12,666 --> 00:14:15,200
and the honest one isn't the one you'd expect

260
00:14:15,900 --> 00:14:20,166
option 1 bring the whole plant in and treat it like a houseplant

261
00:14:20,400 --> 00:14:23,066
cut it back by 1/3 to 1/2 first

262
00:14:23,066 --> 00:14:26,200
water it well and give it a bright window or a lamp

263
00:14:26,300 --> 00:14:29,766
keep it away from heat vents because it likes the room cool

264
00:14:29,866 --> 00:14:32,566
water when the soil feels dry to the touch

265
00:14:32,633 --> 00:14:36,566
if it gets leggy pinch the stems back just above a leaf joint

266
00:14:37,600 --> 00:14:39,933
option 2 is the paper bag

267
00:14:40,033 --> 00:14:43,900
dig the plant up before the frost and shake the soil off the roots

268
00:14:44,100 --> 00:14:45,933
then hang it upside down

269
00:14:46,000 --> 00:14:50,866
or set it in a big paper bag with the top left open so air can move

270
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,666
keep it somewhere cool dark and dry

271
00:14:53,700 --> 00:14:57,600
and above freezing around 45 to 55 degrees

272
00:14:57,866 --> 00:15:00,566
an unheated room an indoor porch

273
00:15:00,566 --> 00:15:04,133
a cool basement or a garage that stays above freezing

274
00:15:05,100 --> 00:15:07,666
expect the leaves to dry up and fall off

275
00:15:07,666 --> 00:15:10,000
don't worry that's normal a trick

276
00:15:10,100 --> 00:15:12,666
take the plant down a few times over the winter

277
00:15:12,800 --> 00:15:16,533
soak the roots for a few hours so they don't dry out completely

278
00:15:16,633 --> 00:15:21,666
in March trim it back to firm green tissue pot it and water it well

279
00:15:22,600 --> 00:15:25,500
maybe you remember a paper sack in a cool basement

280
00:15:25,600 --> 00:15:29,500
plants hanging there waiting out the winter it's still on the list

281
00:15:29,566 --> 00:15:30,466
but one source

282
00:15:30,466 --> 00:15:33,900
warns that storing bare root geraniums can be a challenge

283
00:15:34,066 --> 00:15:38,600
its advice is to try several plants and to take cuttings as a backup

284
00:15:39,366 --> 00:15:42,933
look at what your cuttings really are they're not the extra

285
00:15:42,966 --> 00:15:44,200
they're the insurance

286
00:15:44,300 --> 00:15:48,133
if the bag fails the cutting still carries the plant forward

287
00:15:49,066 --> 00:15:53,133
option 3 is the one you've already started you're a step ahead

288
00:15:53,200 --> 00:15:54,866
when the roots are well developed

289
00:15:54,866 --> 00:15:59,400
move each cutting into its own small pot about 3 or 4 inches wide

290
00:15:59,600 --> 00:16:02,800
give it a sunny window or a lamp for the rest of the winter

291
00:16:03,700 --> 00:16:05,766
one last mistake for the season

292
00:16:05,900 --> 00:16:09,533
moving them outside too soon keep everything indoors

293
00:16:09,833 --> 00:16:13,733
until the threat of frost has passed your zone sets that date

294
00:16:13,866 --> 00:16:17,500
look up your last frost date the same way you looked up the first one

295
00:16:18,300 --> 00:16:20,233
you don't have to pick just one

296
00:16:20,233 --> 00:16:24,466
cuttings on the windowsill and the plant itself in a pot or a bag

297
00:16:24,500 --> 00:16:27,166
if one fails you still have the other

298
00:16:27,233 --> 00:16:31,166
short on room the cuttings alone are enough to keep the plant going

299
00:16:32,233 --> 00:16:35,100
tell us where you keep yours and what zone you're in

300
00:16:35,100 --> 00:16:38,500
it helps everyone else figure out what's realistic where they live

301
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:43,500
a few questions keep coming up under other videos about this

302
00:16:43,500 --> 00:16:45,066
quick answers first

303
00:16:45,833 --> 00:16:50,066
which month before your first frost your zone sets the date

304
00:16:50,066 --> 00:16:53,066
where do they live a sunny window or a lamp

305
00:16:53,066 --> 00:16:56,133
bright but out of direct sun do you need peat

306
00:16:56,266 --> 00:16:59,300
no perlite or sand alone will do

307
00:17:00,166 --> 00:17:04,166
now the open ones Nats when you pot no answer yet

308
00:17:04,166 --> 00:17:07,400
rooting in the final pot or moving later same

309
00:17:07,433 --> 00:17:10,400
and pushing cuttings straight into soil with no bag

310
00:17:10,433 --> 00:17:13,900
one viewer comment says 95% rooted that way

311
00:17:13,900 --> 00:17:17,400
we couldn't verify that number maybe your windowsill can

312
00:17:18,200 --> 00:17:22,266
and if you'd rather skip single use plastic a dome is on the list

313
00:17:22,400 --> 00:17:25,100
whether anything else works we can't say

314
00:17:25,866 --> 00:17:29,466
put your answers in the comments there how this list gets shorter

315
00:17:30,600 --> 00:17:34,600
by spring that windowsill could be a row of small pots

316
00:17:34,766 --> 00:17:36,700
one for every cutting that took

317
00:17:37,633 --> 00:17:40,166
snip it root it pot it up

318
00:17:40,900 --> 00:17:43,366
if you'd rather grow your next geraniums

319
00:17:43,400 --> 00:17:45,700
than buy them you belong here

320
00:17:46,400 --> 00:17:48,366
so take one cutting today

321
00:17:49,300 --> 00:17:50,533
happy gardening

